INT. HOTEL LINEN ROOM - NIGHT

A windowless cupboard of a room, walls lined with shelves of folded white sheets and towels in neat stacks. A bare bulb. A laundry chute in the corner breathes cold air.

PHILOMENA CROSS, 50s, a private investigator in a rumpled raincoat, sits cross-legged on an upturned laundry cart. A pocket recorder in her palm. Her hair is damp.

She presses RECORD.

PHILOMENA (V.O.)
(recorded, hushed)
Tuesday. Two-oh-six a.m. Linen room, fourth floor, the Grand Meridian. The key card the maid gave me opens this door and nothing else.

She stops the tape. Listens to the building: the groan of pipes, a distant lift.

She presses RECORD.

PHILOMENA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
Pencil sketch: eight shelves, eight stacks, one false panel behind the towels on the left.

She stops. Rises. Crosses to the left shelf. Slides the towels to the floor, one by one, with surgical care.

Behind them: a plywood panel, painted to match the wall. A brass latch.

She presses RECORD.

PHILOMENA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
Panel found. Latch is warm.

She stops. Her breath catches. Warm.

Beyond the panel, a soft click. Someone on the other side lets go of the latch.

Philomena freezes. Her thumb finds the recorder's button. Her other hand slides to her coat pocket.

She presses RECORD.

PHILOMENA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded, barely a whisper)
If anyone finds this tape, the Grand Meridian is not a hotel.

The latch turns.

She hurls the recorder into the laundry chute. It clatters down into the dark.

The panel swings inward.
